Z||000020||Tigger||02-27-2001||10:05 AM||||What pressure gradient is required to accelerate kerosene (S=0.81) vertically upward in a vertical pipe at a rate of 0.2g?<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Kerosene acts as an ideal fluid<BR><LI>Specific weight of kerosene y = 51 lbf/ft3<BR><LI>Density p = 1.58 slugs/ft3<BR><LI>g = 32.2 ft/s2<BR><LI>al = 0.2(g) ft/s2<BR><LI>Use Euler equation -(dp/dz)-y(dz/dl) = p(al)<BR></UL><BR>I know I'm giving this one away, but the trick here is (dz/dl)=sin(alpha).  Since the pipe is vertical assume alpha is 90 degrees.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Man, hopefully I didn't kill the thread...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I've got a test over this stuff later tonight.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  Figured I'd share a problem.  (hint: solve for (dp/dz) in the euler eqn.)<P>BTW, don't feel bad, I got 5000  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||10
